RULE "C# Snake Case Private Fields" : IHealerArchitecture

ID: "NAMING-CSHARP-SNAKE-CASE-FIELDS-001"

NAME: "C# Snake Case Private Fields"

DESCRIPTION:
  Private fields using snake_case naming convention instead of C# standard camelCase or _camelCase.
  This violates .NET naming conventions and reduces code readability.

CATEGORY: NamingConventions

SEVERITY: Warning

LANGUAGES:
  - csharp

  C# naming conventions specify that private fields should use camelCase (e.g., apiKey)
  or _camelCase with underscore prefix (e.g., _apiKey). Using snake_case (e.g., api_key)
  is a convention from other languages like Python or Ruby and is inconsistent with
  C# standards.

  This creates:
  - **Code Inconsistency**: Mixing naming styles makes code harder to read
  - **Reduced Maintainability**: Developers expect C# conventions; violations slow comprehension
  - **Team Confusion**: New developers may not recognize the field's scope or purpose
  - **Tooling Issues**: Some refactoring tools expect standard C# naming

BUSINESS_IMPACT: |
  Code Style Violations:
  - Slower code reviews (15-20% more time spent on style discussions)
  - Increased onboarding time for new developers
  - Higher risk of bugs during refactoring due to inconsistent patterns
  - Reduced team velocity from context-switching between naming styles

  Cost Impact:
  - Code review delays: $200-$500 per PR with extensive style violations
  - Onboarding overhead: 5-10 hours additional training per new developer
  - Refactoring errors: $1K-$5K per incident when automated tools miss non-standard names

PLUGIN:
  ID: "csharp-naming-validator"
  CONFIG:
    check_snake_case_fields: true

VIOLATIONS:
  - "Private field uses snake_case instead of camelCase or _camelCase"

EXAMPLES:
  VIOLATION:
    ```csharp
    public class PaymentService
    {
        private string api_key;           // VIOLATION: snake_case
        private int max_retries;          // VIOLATION: snake_case
        private bool use_sandbox_mode;    // VIOLATION: snake_case

        public void ProcessPayment()
        {
            var client = new StripeClient(api_key);
            // ... rest of implementation
        }
    }
    ```

  COMPLIANT:
    ```csharp
    public class PaymentService
    {
        private string _apiKey;           // CORRECT: _camelCase with underscore prefix
        private int _maxRetries;          // CORRECT: _camelCase with underscore prefix
        private bool _useSandboxMode;     // CORRECT: _camelCase with underscore prefix

        public void ProcessPayment()
        {
            var client = new StripeClient(_apiKey);
            // ... rest of implementation
        }
    }
    ```

FIXES:
  PLUGIN:
    ID: "csharp-naming-healer"
    CONFIG:
      use_underscore_prefix: true

  QUICK_FIX: |
    Convert snake_case field names to _camelCase:
    1. Identify the field: private string api_key;
    2. Convert to camelCase: apiKey
    3. Add underscore prefix: _apiKey
    4. Update all references throughout the file

    The TypeScript healer plugin will automatically:
    - Rename the field declaration
    - Update all usages of the field (this.api_key -> this._apiKey)
    - Ensure consistent naming throughout the file

    Example:
      Before: private string api_key;
      After:  private string _apiKey;

EDUCATION:
  LEARN_MORE:
    - title: "C# Naming Conventions"
      url: "https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/csharp/fundamentals/coding-style/coding-conventions"
    - title: ".NET Framework Design Guidelines"
      url: "https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/standard/design-guidelines/naming-guidelines"
    - title: "C# Coding Standards Best Practices"
      url: "https://www.dofactory.com/csharp-coding-standards"

  TRAINING_NOTES: |
    **C# Field Naming Conventions:**

    1. **Private Fields**: Use camelCase or _camelCase (with underscore)
       - camelCase: `private string apiKey;`
       - _camelCase: `private string _apiKey;` (more common in modern C#)

    2. **Public Fields**: Use PascalCase (though public fields are discouraged)
       - PascalCase: `public string ApiKey;`

    3. **Why Underscore Prefix?**
       - Distinguishes fields from local variables and parameters
       - Makes it clear when you're accessing instance state vs. local scope
       - Recommended by Microsoft's C# team for new code

    4. **Migration Strategy:**
       - Use the healer plugin to automatically convert existing code
       - Enforce convention for all new code via real-time validation
       - Update code review checklists to catch manual violations

    **Remember:** Consistency is key. Pick one convention (_camelCase or camelCase)
    and apply it consistently across your codebase.

